      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before Spanish ranchos appeared in the 1760s, the region was first inhabited by Coahuiltecan hunter‑gatherers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/camargito_texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Rancho Carnestolendas and Corrales de los Saenz, founded by the Garza and Saenz families in the 1760s, anchored early Camargito.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/camargito_texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "By the late 1800s ranching foundations transitioned to formal communities, paving the way for 20th‑century irrigation farms fed by the Rio Grande.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/camargito_texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Bracero Program, running from 1942 to 1964, brought Mexican workers to Camargito’s farms, linking the town to wartime labor needs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/camargito_texas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Camargito functions as a census‑designated place within Starr County, lacking its own local government.",
